[{:registry/operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit], :registry/required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger], :actor/scope-excluded-ops [:finalize-grinding-decision :authorize-polishing-operation :proceed-with-grinding-work :override-safety-officer-judgment :override-shop-safety-officer-judgment], :actor/robotics? true, :adr/related ["2799007213-cloud-itonami-isco-7213-sheet-metal-workers-coverage" "2799007222-cloud-itonami-isco-7222-toolmakers-coverage" "2799007221-cloud-itonami-isco-7221-blacksmiths-hammersmiths-and-forging-press-workers-coverage" "2799007211-cloud-itonami-isco-7211-metal-moulders-coremakers-coverage"], :adr/status :accepted, :actor/scope-excluded-terms ["proceed with the grinding operation" "proceed with the grinding work" "proceed with the polishing operation" "proceed with the polishing work" "finalize the grinding decision" "finalize the polishing decision" "authorize the grinding operation" "authorize the polishing operation" "override the shop safety officer's judgment" "override the safety officer's judgment" "override shop safety officer judgment" "override safety officer judgment"], :adr/id 2799007224, :phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:flag-safety-concern :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}}}", :actor/domain :manufacturing/metal-polishing-workshop-scheduling-coordination, :actor/proposal-ops [:log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order], :actor/hard-checks [:worker-verified :workshop-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :scope-exclusion], :registry/maturity :implemented, :actor/isco-08 "7224", :actor/always-escalate-ops [:flag-safety-concern], :adr/doc_type "adr", :operation/nodes [:intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold], :operation/entry-point :intake, :adr/body "# ADR 2799007224: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7224 Metal Polishers, Wheel Grinders and Tool Sharpeners workshop scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7224\n-- Metal Polishers, Wheel Grinders and Tool Sharpeners** only, Wave3\n(production/trades) of the rollout.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7224\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7224\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Metal Polishers, Wheel Grinders\nand Tool Sharpeners\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to\nthis ADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with\n`:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no\n`:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7224` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`7224` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nMetal Polishers, Wheel Grinders and Tool Sharpeners operate abrasive\ngrinding/polishing/sharpening equipment on an active shop floor --\nreal physical-safety stakes (metal-particulate exposure, abrasive-\nwheel-failure hazard, hand/eye injury risk). The controlling\nconstraint for this vertical is that the closed op allowlist must\nNEVER include any op that directly finalizes a grinding/polishing-\nexecution decision (e.g. deciding to proceed with a specific grinding\nor polishing operation), or overrides a shop safety officer's\njudgment -- those decisions must always be a hard permanent block or\nan always-escalate op, never auto-commit-eligible. This actor is\ndeliberately scoped to WORKSHOP SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION\nONLY -- it never performs grinding or polishing work itself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7213`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7213)\n(Sheet Metal Workers: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor` split,\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set, same Wave3 rollout, same\ncutting/finishing physical-safety-domain shape) -- cloned and read in\nfull in this session. The closed allowlist + always-escalate safety\nflag + content-based scope-exclusion with a self-trip regression test\nwas mirrored closely from that same reference, which itself carries\nforward the pattern from `cloud-itonami-isco-9311` (Mining and\nQuarrying Labourers).\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7224` as an OSS workshop\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for metal polishers, wheel\ngrinders and tool sharpeners, following the SAME governed-actor\narchitecture as every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this\nfleet: **Metal Polisher Advisor ⊣ MetalPolisherGovernor** on a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`,\ngit-sha-pinned identically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- a\ndifferent library coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the\nunrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **workshop scheduling/logistics\ncoordination ONLY, never direct grinding/polishing-execution\nauthority** -- it must NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate\nexercising, or propose exercising a grinding/polishing-execution\ndecision (e.g. a specific grinding or polishing operation), or any\noverride of a shop safety officer's judgment. These are not merely\ngated behind escalation -- they are structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- task/materials-usage/progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface an abrasive-wheel-hazard/\n  particulate-exposure/hand-eye-injury concern, ALWAYS escalates,\n  NEVER auto-commit eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- abrasive-materials/tooling procurement\n  proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a\ngrinding/polishing-execution decision or a shop-safety-officer-\njudgment override. These are **structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an op\nsuch as `:finalize-grinding-decision` cannot even be proposed by the\nreference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for` functions only\never emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any code path (a\nforged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever\nproduced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check\n(`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A second,\nindependent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names five\nconcretely-forbidden ops (`metalpolisher.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-grinding-decision`, `:authorize-polishing-operation`,\n`:proceed-with-grinding-work`, `:override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and is\nverified by dedicated unit tests\n(`metalpolisher.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-grinding-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-polishing-operation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-grinding-work`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Worker unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered worker record (`:no-worker`). Never\n   trusts the proposal's own claim of who the worker is.\n2. **Workshop unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an\n   independently registered workshop (`:no-workshop`). Together with\n   check 1, this directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that a\n   worker/workshop record must be independently verified/registered\n   before any action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for proceeding with grinding\n   or polishing work, or overriding a shop safety officer's judgment,\n   is a second HARD, PERMANENT block (`:scope-excluded-action`),\n   evaluated unconditionally on content -- defense-in-depth on top of\n   the allowlist check: even if a future change somehow widened the\n   allowlist, content-based rejection still independently blocks\n   grinding/polishing-execution/safety-override language wherever it\n   appears in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface an abrasive-wheel-\n  hazard/particulate-exposure/hand-eye-injury observation -- it never\n  resolves the concern itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is\n  the hazard data,\" never \"here is what I recommend doing about shop\n  safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an over-threshold\n  abrasive-materials/tooling order is not itself unsafe, unlike a\n  grinding/polishing-execution or safety-officer-override attempt,\n  which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate\n  vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"grinding\", \"polishing\", \"safety\") can accidentally match inside\nthe mock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate\nproposal, self-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly\nthe risk this vertical's own vocabulary (a workshop coordination actor\nworking adjacent to grinding/polishing work necessarily discusses\nthose concepts to do its scheduling/logging job) could trigger if\nphrased carelessly. `metalpolisher.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is\nphrased exclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"proceed\nwith the grinding operation\", \"authorize the polishing operation\",\n\"override the shop safety officer's judgment\" -- matching the\ntask's own worked example phrasing), never bare nouns, and\n`metalpolisher.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nwork record for worker ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for grinding\ntask ...\", \"... routed for shop safety officer review\") WITHOUT\ncontaining any of the full finalization-action phrases, so the\nregression test is a genuine stress test of the bare-noun-vs-action-\nphrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on first run in this\nsession -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never\ntripped during development.\n`metalpolisher.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nadditionally exercises the same invariant through the full graph run,\nconfirming a scope-excluded proposal never reaches `commit-record!`\nregardless of disposition path.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7224\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7224` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7224`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 21 tests, 45 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-grinding-decision` or\n  `:approve-polishing-operation` that is merely gated behind an\n  always-escalate list: rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  domain-design guidance -- an always-escalate op still means the\n  *capability* to finalize a grinding/polishing-execution decision\n  exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For\n  a decision this severe (abrasive-wheel-failure hazard, particulate\n  exposure), the capability itself must not exist. The only op related\n  to shop-safety escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces\n  the need for human/shop-safety-officer attention and never itself\n  decides, authorizes, or defers a grinding/polishing decision for\n  later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring earlier\n  reference actors' transaction-amount-ceiling HARD checks): rejected\n  per the task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold abrasive-\n  materials/tooling order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a\n  soft escalate gate, not a hard block; only the grinding/polishing-\n  execution/safety-officer-override class is a hard, unconditional\n  block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"grinding\"\n  \"polishing\" \"safety\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known\n  self-trip bug class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked\n  example for this exact task), and per the explicit instruction to\n  phrase scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS,\n  never bare nouns -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses\n  grinding/polishing/safety-officer concepts to do its\n  scheduling/logging job, so a bare-noun list would risk\n  self-blocking the actor's own legitimate proposals\n  (regression-tested directly against rationale text that\n  deliberately contains those bare nouns).\n- Merging `worker` and `workshop` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered worker AND independently-registered\n  workshop) matches the CRITICAL invariant that a \"worker/workshop\n  record must be independently verified/registered before any action\"\n  and the established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors\n  with two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- task/materials-usage/progress data\n   logging only. Requires an independently registered worker AND\n   workshop (`:no-worker`/`:no-workshop`). Not a grinding/polishing-\n   execution or safety-officer decision in any respect -- it is a\n   records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n   only. Cannot carry a grinding/polishing-execution decision\n   (structurally absent from the op's field shape; any such attempt\n   via free text is caught by the `:scope-excluded-action` content\n   check). Does not authorize, finalize, or draw any conclusion about\n   a specific grinding or polishing step -- it is a\n   calendar/logistics action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces an abrasive-wheel-hazard/\n   particulate-exposure/hand-eye-injury concern to a human/shop safety\n   officer; ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally, at every confidence\n   level, NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY channel through\n   which a safety observation may be surfaced, and it explicitly never\n   resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself -- the shop safety\n   officer reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- abrasive-materials/tooling\n   procurement proposal only (item, cost). Not a grinding/polishing-\n   execution or authorization decision of any kind -- it is a\n   purchasing-logistics action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling\n   escalation (above 2000 always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na grinding/polishing-execution decision (e.g. proceeding with a\nspecific grinding or polishing operation) or a shop-safety-officer-\njudgment override -- these categories are structurally absent, not\ngated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself\n(`metalpolisher.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) five concretely-named\nforbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit level\n(`metalpolisher.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`metalpolisher.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-grinding-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-polishing-operation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-grinding-work`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`),\nand (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the\n`:op` field (`metalpolisher.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale\nthat attempts to proceed with grinding work or override a shop safety\nofficer's judgment even via an otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`metalpolisher.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-shop-safety-officer`).\n", :adr/date "2026-07-18", :actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 2000}", :test/counts "{:total 21, :assertions 45, :failures 0, :errors 0}", :actor/coordination? true, :actor/name "Metal Polishing Workshop Scheduling Coordination Practice", :db/id -1, :test/coverage [:governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :worker-verification :workshop-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation], :registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-7224", :registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7224", :adr/scope [:kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7224], :test/simulator :metalpolisher.actor-test, :adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-7224 — Metal Polishers, Wheel Grinders and Tool Sharpeners Workshop Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage", :adr/source-format "edn", :adr/references [2607121000], :actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-7224}]
